---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import DocsSidebar from "../components/DocsSidebar.astro";
---

<Layout title="Getting started · Succulent" activeNav="guide">
	<div class="docs">
		<DocsSidebar active="getting-started" />

		<!-- MAIN -->
		<main class="docs-main">
			<article class="prose">
				<div class="crumb">
					Docs <span class="sep">/</span> Introduction
					<span class="sep">/</span> Getting started
				</div>
				<h1>Getting started</h1>
				<p class="sub">
					Install Succulent, write your first guard, and go from
					<code class="inl">unknown</code> to a fully-typed value in about two
					minutes.
				</p>

				<div class="callout">
					<span class="i">$</span>
					<p>
						<strong>The one idea:</strong> every schema is named after the
						TypeScript type it represents, prefixed with a
						<code class="inl">$</code>. The schema for
						<code class="inl">string</code> is
						<code class="inl">$string</code>. Learn that, and you already know
						most of the library.
					</p>
				</div>

				<h2 id="motivation">Why succulent?</h2>
				<p>
					A lot of validation libraries ask you to learn a second dialect — new
					names, new syntax, subtle differences from the TypeScript you write
					every day. Succulent's goal is the opposite: make runtime validation
					feel like <em>just writing TypeScript</em>, and make the necessary
					differences obvious fast.
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<strong>It's just TypeScript.</strong>
						<code class="inl">string</code> →
						<code class="inl">$string</code>, <code class="inl">Date</code> →
						<code class="inl">$Date</code>, <code class="inl">T[]</code> →
						<code class="inl">$Array($T)</code>.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Assert or check.</strong>
						<code class="inl">guard()</code> narrows and throws;
						<code class="inl">is()</code> returns a boolean.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Composable filters.</strong> Refine any schema with
						<code class="inl">.that(matches(/…/), inRange(0, 10))</code>.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Types for free.</strong> Generate a matching type with
						<code class="inl">Type&lt;typeof $User&gt;</code>.
					</li>
				</ul>

				<h2 id="install">Installation</h2>
				<p>
					Succulent is a zero-dependency ESM package. Install it with your
					package manager of choice:
				</p>
				<div class="code">
					<div class="code-head">
						<span class="fname">terminal</span>
						<button class="copy">
							<svg
								viewBox="0 0 24 24"
								width="13"
								height="13"
								fill="none"
								stroke="currentColor"
								stroke-width="2"
							>
								<rect x="9" y="9" width="11" height="11" rx="2"></rect>
								<path d="M5 15V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h10"></path>
							</svg>
							<span class="copy-label">copy</span>
						</button>
					</div>
					<pre><code><span class="tok-schema">$</span> npm install succulent
<span class="tok-comment"># or: pnpm add succulent · yarn add succulent · bun add succulent</span></code></pre>
				</div>

				<hr />

				<h2 id="first-guard">Your first guard</h2>
				<p>
					Say you have a value of type <code class="inl">unknown</code> — a
					request body, some parsed JSON, anything you didn't create yourself.
					Pass it to <code class="inl">guard</code> along with a schema. If it
					matches, TypeScript narrows the type. If it doesn't,
					<code class="inl">guard</code> throws a
					<code class="inl">CheckError</code> (which extends
					<code class="inl">TypeError</code>).
				</p>
				<div class="code">
					<div class="code-head">
						<span class="fname">clean.ts</span>
						<button class="copy">
							<svg
								viewBox="0 0 24 24"
								width="13"
								height="13"
								fill="none"
								stroke="currentColor"
								stroke-width="2"
							>
								<rect x="9" y="9" width="11" height="11" rx="2"></rect>
								<path d="M5 15V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h10"></path>
							</svg>
							<span class="copy-label">copy</span>
						</button>
					</div>
					<pre data-lang="ts"><code>{`import { guard, $string } from "succulent";

export default function (x: unknown): string {
  guard(x, $string);

  // x now has type \`string\`, and can be treated as such
  return x;
}`}</code></pre>
				</div>
				<p>
					Prefer a boolean instead of a thrown error? Use
					<code class="inl">is</code>, which narrows inside the branch:
				</p>
				<pre class="bare"><code data-lang="ts">{`import { is, $number } from "succulent";

if (is(value, $number)) {
  // value is a number in here
}`}</code></pre>

				<h2 id="objects">Validating objects</h2>
				<p>
					Describe object shapes with <code class="inl">$interface</code>. It
					takes a template whose values are themselves schemas — exactly
					mirroring how you'd write the type.
				</p>
				<div class="code">
					<div class="code-head">
						<span class="fname">user.ts</span>
						<button class="copy">
							<svg
								viewBox="0 0 24 24"
								width="13"
								height="13"
								fill="none"
								stroke="currentColor"
								stroke-width="2"
							>
								<rect x="9" y="9" width="11" height="11" rx="2"></rect>
								<path d="M5 15V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h10"></path>
							</svg>
							<span class="copy-label">copy</span>
						</button>
					</div>
					<pre data-lang="ts"><code>{`import { $interface, $optional, $string, $Date } from "succulent";

export const $User = $interface({
  name: $string,
  meta: $optional($interface({
    lastSeen: $optional($Date),
  })),
});`}</code></pre>
				</div>

				<h2 id="filters">Refining with filters</h2>
				<p>
					Every schema has a <code class="inl">.that()</code> method that takes
					one or more filters — small predicate functions. Chain as many as you
					need.
				</p>
				<pre
					class="bare"><code data-lang="ts">{`import { matches, maxLength, inRange, $string, $int } from "succulent";

const $Handle = $string.that(matches(/^[a-z0-9_]+$/), maxLength(15));
const $Score  = $int.that(inRange(0, 100));`}</code></pre>
				<div class="callout clay">
					<span class="i">!</span>
					<p>
						Filters run <em>after</em> the base type check, so inside
						<code class="inl">inRange</code> you can safely assume you already
						have a number.
					</p>
				</div>

				<h2 id="types">Inferring types</h2>
				<p>
					Don't write the type twice. Derive it from the schema with
					<code class="inl">Type</code>, and the two can never drift out of
					sync.
				</p>
				<pre
					class="bare"><code data-lang="ts">{`import { Type } from "succulent";

export type User = Type<typeof $User>;
// equivalently: export interface User extends Type<typeof $User> {}`}</code></pre>

				<h2 id="recursive">Recursive types</h2>
				<p>
					For self-referential shapes, wrap the recursive reference in
					<code class="inl">lazy</code> so it can refer to a schema that isn't
					defined yet.
				</p>
				<pre
					class="bare"><code data-lang="ts">{`import { inRange, lazy, Schema, $Array, $int, $interface, $string } from "succulent";

type Friend = { name: string; happiness: number; friends: Friend[] };

const $Friend: Schema<Friend> = $interface({
  name: $string,
  happiness: $int.that(inRange(0, 10)),
  friends: $Array(lazy(() => $Friend)),
});`}</code></pre>

				<h2 id="errors">Handling errors</h2>
				<p>
					When <code class="inl">guard</code> fails it throws a
					<code class="inl">CheckError</code> with a traced hierarchy, so you
					can see exactly which property was invalid. Catch with
					<code class="inl">CheckError</code> when you want to distinguish
					succulent's failures from other <code class="inl">TypeError</code>s.
				</p>
				<pre class="bare"><code data-lang="ts">{`import { guard, CheckError } from "succulent";

try {
  guard(person, $Friend);
  // person has type \`Friend\` now!
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof CheckError) {
    // Probe the hierarchy of where the error came from
  }
}`}</code></pre>

				<h2 id="namespace">The <code class="inl">$</code> namespace</h2>
				<p>
					If you'd rather not import every schema individually — or you're
					coming from zod and miss the <code class="inl">z.string()</code> style
					— Succulent also exports a single <code class="inl">$</code> object
					that bundles every built-in schema:
				</p>
				<pre class="bare"><code data-lang="ts">{`import { $, guard, inRange } from "succulent";

const $User = $.type({
  id: $.string,
  age: $.int.that(inRange(0, 120)),
  friends: $.Array($.string),
});

guard(unknownInput, $User);`}</code></pre>
				<p>
					The naming follows the underlying schema with the leading
					<code class="inl">$</code> dropped, so
					<code class="inl">$string</code> becomes
					<code class="inl">$.string</code>,
					<code class="inl">$Array</code> becomes
					<code class="inl">$.Array</code>, and so on.
					<code class="inl">$interface</code> is available as both
					<code class="inl">$.interface</code> and
					<code class="inl">$.type</code> — pick whichever reads better in
					context.
				</p>
				<div class="callout">
					<span class="i">$</span>
					<p>
						<code class="inl">$</code> only collects the type schemas. Operators
						(<code class="inl">guard</code>, <code class="inl">is</code>,
						<code class="inl">union</code>, <code class="inl">lazy</code>, …) and
						filters (<code class="inl">matches</code>,
						<code class="inl">inRange</code>, …) are still standalone imports —
						mix and match whichever style you like.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="pager">
					<a href=".">
						<div class="dir">←</div>
						<div class="ttl">Home</div>
					</a>
					<a class="next" href="api">
						<div class="dir">→</div>
						<div class="ttl">API reference</div>
					</a>
				</div>
			</article>
		</main>

		<!-- ON THIS PAGE -->
		<aside class="docs-toc">
			<h5>On this page</h5>
			<nav id="toc">
				<a href="#motivation">Why succulent?</a>
				<a href="#install">Installation</a>
				<a href="#first-guard">Your first guard</a>
				<a href="#objects">Validating objects</a>
				<a href="#filters">Refining with filters</a>
				<a href="#types">Inferring types</a>
				<a href="#recursive">Recursive types</a>
				<a href="#errors">Handling errors</a>
				<a href="#namespace">The $ namespace</a>
			</nav>
		</aside>
	</div>

	<script is:inline>
		// active TOC highlight
		(function () {
			var links = [].slice.call(document.querySelectorAll("#toc a"));
			var heads = links.map(function (a) {
				return document.querySelector(a.getAttribute("href"));
			});
			function onScroll() {
				var y = window.scrollY + 100,
					cur = 0;
				heads.forEach(function (h, i) {
					if (h && h.offsetTop <= y) cur = i;
				});
				links.forEach(function (a, i) {
					a.classList.toggle("active", i === cur);
				});
			}
			window.addEventListener("scroll", onScroll);
			onScroll();
		})();
	</script>
</Layout>
